 > **Note on inference range.** Our method does not perform state resetting by default, so the maximum inference range is bounded by the longest distance seen during training on the dataset. Beyond that distance, state resetting becomes necessary. If you observe pose collapse, switch to windowed mode (`--mode windowed`) — in most cases tuning `--keyframe_interval` alone is enough and the rest of the windowed parameters can stay at their defaults.
 
 
+### Bounded Context / Indefinite Streaming (`--max_context_frames`)
+
+Streaming state has three parts, and by default only two of them are bounded:
+
+| Part | Holds | Bounded by | Cost at 518x294 |
+|:--|:--|:--|:--|
+| Anchor context | first `--num_scale_frames` frames, full detail | never evicted | ~0.6 GB |
+| Local pose-reference window | last N keyframes, full detail | `--kv_cache_sliding_window` | ~77 MB per frame |
+| Trajectory memory | 6 special tokens per past keyframe | **nothing, by default** | ~0.3 MB per frame |
+
+The trajectory memory is append-only, so KV memory grows for the entire
+sequence and an open-ended run eventually OOMs no matter how small the window
+is. `--max_context_frames N` caps it: each frame then sees at most the anchor,
+the local window, and the newest `N` frames of trajectory memory, and total KV
+memory becomes **constant**.
+
+```bash
+python demo.py --model_path /path/to/lingbot-map.pt \
+    --video_path video.mp4 --fps 10 \
+    --max_context_frames 256 --kv_cache_sliding_window 64
+```
+
+Unlike `--mode windowed`, nothing is reset: the cache is a rolling working set,
+so there are no window seams to stitch or align. The trade is that geometry
+older than `N` keyframes stops contributing to drift correction.
+
+- Default is `-1` (unbounded, original behavior).
+- On the SDPA backend the cap is exact. On FlashInfer, eviction is page-granular
+  (`floor(page_size / 6)` frames), so the live memory oscillates between `N` and
+  `N + frames_per_special_page`; the first special page is pinned as an
+  attention sink so the anchor's special tokens survive.
+- `--kv_cache_sliding_window` dominates VRAM (~77 MB/frame at 518x294 vs
+  ~0.3 MB/frame for trajectory memory) — reach for it first when tuning.
+- Sizing guide (518x294, SDPA, bf16): window 32 ~= 4 GB, 64 ~= 7 GB, 96 ~= 10 GB,
+  plus ~4.6 GB of model weights.
+
+3D-RoPE positions still advance once per **keyframe**, so `--max_frame_num`
+bounds total keyframes (~512 B per slot). Raise it, or raise
+`--keyframe_interval` so each slot covers more frames.
+
+### Open-Ended Runs with a Live Viewer (`stream_video.py`)
+
+`demo.py` decodes the whole video up front and accumulates every frame's dense
+prediction before opening the viewer, so RAM grows with length even when the KV
+cache does not. `stream_video.py` keeps ingest, inference, and output all flat:
+frames are decoded one at a time, predictions are consumed immediately into a
+voxel-hashed global map with a hard point budget, and the viser viewer comes up
+*before* inference and refreshes as the map grows.
+
+```bash
+python stream_video.py \
+    --video_path video.mp4 --fps 10 \
+    --model_path /path/to/lingbot-map.pt \
+    --max_context_frames 256 --kv_cache_sliding_window 64 \
+    --max_points 3000000 --port 8080
+```
+
+| Flag | Purpose |
+|:--|:--|
+| `--max_context_frames` | Trajectory-memory cap (above) |
+| `--max_points` | Hard ceiling on map points; oldest voxels age out |
+| `--voxel_size` | Map dedup resolution (0 = auto from the initial scene extent) |
+| `--loop` | Replay the source forever — the memory-stability soak test |
+| `--save_ply` | Write the final map on exit |
+| `--no_viewer` | Headless (benchmarking) |
+
+Per-frame stats report VRAM, map size, and live trajectory-memory depth, so you
+can watch the footprint flatten instead of trusting that it does.
+
